They were exported from flyvis and its published pretrained models and
reformatted; the reference traces were produced by running flyvis's
models. The provenance JSON files beside the fixtures record how. The
pretrained models carry no licence statement of their own; the paper
states that its trained models are available from the flyvis repository,
whose licence is the MIT licence above.

The fixtures contain no MPI Sintel imagery. The stimuli are synthetic
(flashes, dots, moving edges), generated by flyvis's own code.

The connectome spec the tests read,
data/flyvis-supplementary/Lappalainen_et_al_connectome_avg_filters.json,
is not redistributed here: ci/fetch-fixtures.sh downloads it from the
paper's Supplementary Information on Springer's site.

Model and data source:
  Lappalainen, J. K. et al. Connectome-constrained networks predict
  neural activity across the fly visual system. Nature 634, 1132-1140
  (2024). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07939-3
  (CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)

The connectome underlying flyvis derives from the Janelia FlyEM FIB-25
and FIB-19 datasets:
  Takemura, S. et al. Synaptic circuits and their variations within
    different columns in the visual system of Drosophila. PNAS 112,
    13711-13716 (2015). https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1509820112
  Takemura, S. et al. A visual motion detection circuit suggested by
    Drosophila connectomics. Nature 500, 175-181 (2013).
    https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12450
  Shinomiya, K. et al. Comparisons between the ON- and OFF-edge motion
    pathways in the Drosophila brain. eLife 8, e40025 (2019).
    https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.40025  (CC BY 4.0)
